   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general municipal law, in relation to the local\nadministration of empire zones",
    "basePrintNo": "S7946",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7946-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §963, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7946\n\nSPONSOR: MARCHIONE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general municipal law, in relation\nto the local administration of empire zones\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe proposed legislation amends the General Municipal Law to remove the\nrequirement that businesses whose QEZE benefit period has ended must\nfile business annual reports with the Department.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the proposed legislation amends the general municipal law\nto require only business still receiving QEZE benefits to have to submit\nbusiness annual reports to the Department.\n\nSection 2 of the proposed legislation provides that the proposed legis-\nlation is to take effect immediately upon the effective date of the\nbill.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe bill would eliminate an unnecessary reporting requirement and save\ntaxpayers the staff time and mailing costs associated with ensuring\ncompliance for certified businesses no longer earning program benefits.\nBusinesses that fail to report are decertified.\n\nThe Empire Zones Program was closed to new entrants on June 29, 2010.\nNonetheless, businesses certified by the Department of Economic Develop-\nment on or before this date could still earn tax benefits as a qualified\nempire zone enterprise (QEZE) until their benefit periods expired.\nCurrently, there are over 4,500 businesses still certified.  In 2015,\nthe QEZE benefit period expired for over 1,100 businesses certified in\n2001 or earlier. The benefit period expires for another 700 additional\nbusinesses certified in 2002. Each year, the benefit period expires for\nmore businesses until the end of 2019 when virtually every business will\nhave \"timed out\" of the QEZE Program.\n\nHowever, per tax law, some of these businesses must remain certified in\norder to use \"carry-forward\" wage or investment credits earned in earli-\ner years so they cannot ignore the report because, per general municipal\nlaw, the annual report must be filed to avoid decertification.\n\nThis bill would immediately eliminate the business annual reporting\nrequirement for thousands of businesses certified in 2001 and 2002, then\neach year for the next three years, several thousands of additional\nbusinesses would no longer be required to submit reports.\n\nSince the only benefits any certified business can continue to earn are\nthe QEZE benefits, the reporting requirement will not provide any useful\ninformation about the Program for businesses whose QEZE benefit period\nhas expired. The bill would allow these businesses to remain certified\nin order to continue claiming \"carry-forward\" investment or wage credits\nearned years earlier without having to also complete the annual report\nand risk decertification for non-compliance.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
